% ============================================================================
% galaxy-types — the main types of galaxy and the defining shape / description
% each one is known by (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of extragalactic astronomy, and a rung an ASTRO
% agent reasons UP from: the galaxies we see are not a formless zoo — nearly all
% of them sort into a handful of MAIN types by their SHAPE, and each type has a
% characteristic shape it is defined by: a spiral galaxy is the one with spiral
% arms; an elliptical galaxy is a smooth ball that ranges from round to oval; an
% irregular galaxy is the one with no tidy shape at all. (That summary used to
% sit in quotation marks; it is a paraphrase, not the page's words.) Before
% you can reason
% about a galaxy's age, its star formation, or its history you must first know
% WHICH main type you are looking at. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? galaxy_shape(spiral, $Shape)      % spiral_arms
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `galaxy_shape(type, shape)` carrying its row's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the shape
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word that
% is not one of these main galaxy types (it never invents a shape).
%
% Each main galaxy type and the defining shape / description NASA states for it,
% as a little truth table:
%
%     galaxy type      defining shape / description   a beginner's cue
%     -------------    ----------------------------   -------------------------
%     spiral           spiral_arms                    the pinwheel with arms
%     barred_spiral    bar_across_center              a spiral with a central bar
%     elliptical       round_to_oval                  a smooth ball, round to oval
%     lenticular       disk_no_arms                    a disk like a spiral, no arms
%     irregular        unusual_shapes                 no tidy shape at all
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a shape and recall its galaxy type:
%
%     ? galaxy_shape($Type, round_to_oval)   % elliptical
%
% A word that is NOT one of these main galaxy types — `planet`, `star`,
% `nebula` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a galaxy recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing a shape. That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the type→shape pairs it
% can ground, and only those.
%
% The `shape` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% defining shape / description NASA EXPLICITLY states for that type — never one
% the source does not support. (Each atom uses the source's own words — NASA
% says spiral arms, says "round to oval", says a bar that cuts "across their
% centers", says lenticulars have "disk ... but no arms", says irregulars have
% "unusual shapes" — so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted
% span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every type→shape pair below
% was read out of a fetched NASA Science page, and any pair whose shape could not
% be verified there would have been dropped). Each pair, with the primary source
% it was copied from and the verbatim span that states it:
%
%   spiral → spiral_arms
%     https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/types/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Our Milky Way is one example of a broad class of galaxies defined by the
%      presence of spiral arms."
%
%   barred_spiral → bar_across_center
%     https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/types/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Barred spirals sport ribbons of stars, gas, and dust that cut across
%      their centers."
%
%   elliptical → round_to_oval
%     https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/types/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Elliptical galaxies have shapes that range from completely round to
%      oval."
%
%   lenticular → disk_no_arms
%     https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/types/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "They have the central bulge and disk common to spiral galaxies but no
%      arms." ("They" is the sentence before it: "Lenticular galaxies are
%      a kind of cross between spirals and ellipticals.")
%
%   irregular → unusual_shapes
%     https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/types/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Irregular galaxies have unusual shapes, like toothpicks, rings, or even
%      little groupings of stars."
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). This paragraph
% used to say an ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the spiral
% sentence was the `source` of all five answers while the other spans were
% only listed above. Row blocks now let each row carry the span quoted for
% it above; the lenticular row carries both sentences, since "They have
% ..." alone names no type. The envelope is now the page's introduction to
% its types, which names no type and no shape value:
%
%     "Scientists sometimes categorize galaxies based on their shapes and
%      physical features."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the five row spans and the envelope each occur
% exactly once in the page body, each inside a `<p>`. (The page's `<head>`
% metadata repeats the envelope as its description; the row spans occur
% nowhere else in the file.) Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row
% restates a `locator` line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4).
% All sources are NASA Science, a primary U.S. government (NASA) resource, so
% `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table galaxy_shape {
    columns type, shape

    row (spiral, spiral_arms) {
        source "Our Milky Way is one example of a broad class of galaxies defined by the presence of spiral arms."
    }
    row (barred_spiral, bar_across_center) {
        source "Barred spirals sport ribbons of stars, gas, and dust that cut across their centers."
    }
    row (elliptical, round_to_oval) {
        source "Elliptical galaxies have shapes that range from completely round to oval."
    }
    row (lenticular, disk_no_arms) {
        source "Lenticular galaxies are a kind of cross between spirals and ellipticals. They have the central bulge and disk common to spiral galaxies but no arms."
    }
    row (irregular, unusual_shapes) {
        source "Irregular galaxies have unusual shapes, like toothpicks, rings, or even little groupings of stars."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE SPIRAL ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of
    % all five answers. It is now a framing sentence that names no type and
    % no shape. Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "Scientists sometimes categorize galaxies based on their shapes and physical features."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/types/"
    trust authoritative
}
